WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.575
The payments database runs on three nodes.

2
00:00:05.575 --> 00:00:11.119
Each has its own CPUs, memory and disk, and its disk holds two of six partitions.

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:13.515
They share nothing but the network.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.521
Month end needs more CPU.

5
00:00:15.521 --> 00:00:22.076
Add node 4, and before it can help, two partitions, 400 gigabytes, must be copied onto it.

6
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:24.377
Removing it later moves them back.

7
00:00:24.377 --> 00:00:27.025
Compute and data grow and shrink together.

8
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:28.181
Now split them.

9
00:00:28.181 --> 00:00:31.720
The data moves to a storage service on its own machines.

10
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.637
Compute nodes read it over the network, and keep only a cache.

11
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.896
Now adding compute moves no data.

12
00:00:37.896 --> 00:00:42.597
A new node reads what it needs and helps at once, and later it just stops.

13
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:46.341
Now the storage service holds the only durable copy.

14
00:00:46.341 --> 00:00:52.871
It must survive a dead disk, even a lost availability zone: a separate site with its own power.

15
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.841
Amazon's Aurora does this.

16
00:00:54.841 --> 00:01:01.604
It cuts the data into 10 gigabyte segments, and keeps six copies of each, two in each of three zones.

17
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:06.508
On a commit, the database sends only a short log record, never whole pages.

18
00:01:06.508 --> 00:01:11.283
The write counts once four copies have it, and the slow two catch up later.

19
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.159
A read asks three copies.

20
00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:20.324
Any three overlap any four, since three plus four is more than six, so a read always finds the newest write.

21
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:22.040
Now a whole zone fails.

22
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.709
Two copies are gone, four are left, and writes carry on.

23
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.695
Then the disk under c2 dies.

24
00:01:27.695 --> 00:01:32.953
With three left, writes stop, but reads still work, and nothing committed is lost.

25
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:39.955
A new copy of the 10 gigabyte segment takes about ten seconds, and with four copies again, writes resume.

26
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:44.585
Put the durable copy in a storage service, and compute can come and go.

27
00:01:44.585 --> 00:01:48.692
Always ask where the durable copy lives, and what is only a cache.
